1. What is a DoS / DDoS Attack?
Denial of Service (DoS)

An attack that floods a system with traffic so legitimate users cannot access the service.

Distributed Denial of Service (DDoS)

Same idea, but the traffic comes from many different systems at the same time.

📌 Key difference:

DoS → single source

DDoS → multiple sources (distributed)

2. Objective of a DDoS Attack

Attackers aim to:

Make a website/server unavailable

Disrupt business operations

Cause financial loss

Damage reputation

Distract security teams from other attacks

3. How a DDoS Attack Works (Conceptual)
Multiple infected systems
        ↓
   Target server
        ↓
Server resources exhausted
        ↓
Legitimate users blocked

These infected systems are usually part of a botnet.

4. What is a Botnet?

A botnet is a network of:

Compromised PCs

Servers

IoT devices

Each infected device = bot (zombie)
Controller = botmaster

Botnets are commonly used in DDoS attacks.

5. Types of DDoS Attacks
1️⃣ Volume-Based Attacks

Goal: Consume bandwidth

Examples:

UDP flood

ICMP flood

Effect:

Network becomes congested

Legitimate traffic can’t get through

2️⃣ Protocol Attacks

Goal: Exhaust server/network resources

Target:

Firewalls

Load balancers

Network stack

Examples:

SYN flood

Ping of Death

3️⃣ Application-Layer Attacks

Goal: Crash applications

Target:

Web servers

APIs

Login pages

Examples:

HTTP request floods

Slow request attacks

📌 Hardest to detect because traffic looks “normal”.

6. DDoS vs DoS (Comparison)
Feature	DoS	DDoS
Source	Single system	Multiple systems
Power	Low	Very high
Detection	Easier	Harder
Impact	Limited	Massive
7. Signs of a DDoS Attack

🚩 Website extremely slow
🚩 Server timeout errors
🚩 High CPU / memory usage
🚩 Unusual traffic spikes
🚩 Legitimate users blocked

8. Real-World DDoS Targets

E-commerce websites

Banks

Government portals

Gaming servers

Social media platforms

DDoS attacks are often used during:

Political conflicts

Business rivalries

Hacktivism campaigns

9. Why DDoS is Hard to Stop

Traffic comes from many IPs

Often global distribution

Spoofed packets

Legitimate-looking requests

10. DDoS Defense & Mitigation
Network-Level Protection

✔ Firewalls
✔ Rate limiting
✔ Traffic filtering

Application-Level Protection

✔ Web Application Firewalls (WAF)
✔ CAPTCHA
✔ Request validation

Cloud-Based Protection

✔ DDoS mitigation services
✔ Content Delivery Networks (CDN)
✔ Traffic scrubbing centers

Monitoring & Response

✔ Traffic baselining
✔ IDS / IPS
✔ SIEM correlation
✔ Incident response plans

11. Legal & Ethical View

🚫 Launching DDoS attacks is illegal
🚫 Even “testing” without permission is a crime
✅ Learning is allowed for:

Defense

Detection

Exams

SOC roles

12. DDoS in Ethical Hacking

In authorized testing:

Only simulation

Only written permission

Focus on resilience, not damage
